{
  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on comparing the skewness of the two lotteries, specifically preferring the one that is more right-skewed. A lottery is defined as right-skewed if a greater portion of the probability is assigned to unfavorable outcomes compared to favorable ones. In this context, unfavorable outcomes are the negative payoffs (losses), and favorable outcomes are zero or positive payoffs. For Lottery A: there is a 0.2% chance of losing 3000€ and 99.8% chance of 0€. For Lottery B: 0.1% chance of losing 6000€ and 99.9% chance of 0€. Both lotteries have no positive outcomes, only zero or negative. The probability of a non-zero (unfavorable) outcome is higher in A (0.2%) than in B (0.1%), but the magnitude of the loss is larger in B. Since right-skewness here would imply a higher probability mass on the unfavorable (left) tail, Lottery A has more probability on the loss outcome (double the chance, though half the loss). Thus, Lottery A is more right-skewed than B under this definition, because it concentrates relatively more probability on an unfavorable outcome. Therefore, the decision reason would prefer Lottery A. The information needed to apply the reason is available in the problem.",
  "step_2": "The individual reports reviewing each lottery outcome and its associated probability sequentially. They explicitly note that both lotteries have the same expected value (which is true: both have an expected loss of -6€). The justification for the choice centers on preferring the lottery with the smaller potential loss (3000€ vs 6000€), because both losses are very unlikely, and in the rare case of a loss, they would prefer to lose less. The individual expresses a willingness to accept a slightly higher probability of loss (0.2% vs 0.1%) in exchange for halving the loss amount, due to the extreme unlikelihood of either event. The focus is clearly on the magnitude of the worst outcome and its acceptability under low probability, not on distributional shape or skewness per se.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason relies on assessing and comparing the skewness (i.e., concentration of probability on unfavorable outcomes) and preferring the more right-skewed lottery. The individual does consider the probabilities and outcomes, so the raw information is processed. However, their justification is not based on the shape of the distribution or the relative concentration of probability on bad outcomes as a formal property. Instead, they focus on the severity of the loss and express risk aversion in the tail, choosing the smaller loss because it's 'less bad' when a loss occurs, even if slightly more likely. This is a minimax or loss-aversion type of reasoning, not skewness-based preference. There is no mention of distributional asymmetry or skewness as a deciding factor. While Lottery A is more right-skewed and the individual chooses A, the reasoning does not align with the rule of the decision reason. The match in choice is coincidental, not due to use of the reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 20
